## ABSTRACT
## KEYWORDS
${{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{U}}}^{+}$ active sites, metal-organic frameworks,photo-switchable property, azobenzene,adsorptive desulfurizatior
## Introduction
The burning of fossil fuels promotes industrial and economic development, but it also inevitably causes a negative impact on the environment. During the combustion of transportation fuels [1,2], $\mathrm{SO}_{x}$ converted by sulfur is the prime culprit of acid rain and smog [3,4]. Therefore, deep desulfurization is the hot topic at present and governments have established strict regulations to limit sulfur content in fuels. Nowadays various methods, such as hydrodesulfurization (HDS), oxidative desulfurization (ODS), and adsorptive desulfurization (ADS), have been exploited. Although HDS could efficiently remove thiols and sulfides [5], it is less effective for aromatic sulfur compounds such as thiophene, benzothiophene, and their derivatives [6]. For the technology of ODS, aromatic sulfur compounds can be oxidized to sulfones or sulfone compounds by adding an oxidant (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rangle$ ， which can remove aromatic sulfur efficiently [7,8]. However, the method cannot be continuously operated and needs to use relative expensive oxidants [9,10]. Among various alternative technologies for deep desulfurization, ADS has attracted much attention because of its high selectivity for aromatic sulfur compounds at mild conditions [ll]. To date, many kinds of adsorbents have been employed for ADS, especially the ones decorated with $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ sites owing to $\pi$ -complexation interaction with aromatic sulfur compounds [12,13]. This unique characteristicendowsthem outstanding  desulfurization performance with high selectivity for aromatic sulfur compounds [14,15]. Meanwhile, it is known that regeneration of adsorbents plays a significant role in pratical applications. To achieve this, the variation of temperature and/or pressure has been used to regulate the adsorption capacity [16]. However, this approach is usually energy-consuming, especially for those adsorbents with relatively strong adsorption active sites [17,18]. Hence, developing an energy-saving and effective method to regulate adsorption capacity is full of interests.
Recently, stimuli (e.g., pH, temperature, and light) responsive metal-organic frameworks(MOFs) [19,20], with the pore microenvironment altered intelligently by external stimuli, have aroused intensive interests among researchers. Among these stimuli, light has high precision, fast responsiveness, and almost no by-products produced in the process [21, 22]. Such advantages make photo-switchable MOFs (PMOFs) become a promising platform for controlable capture and release of guests [23]. Azobenzene, as one of the most attractive light-responsive molecules, can undergo reversible trans/cis isomerization through photo-irradiation and lead to obvious changes in the structure and polarity [24,25]. Hence, the PMOFs decorated by azobenzene with interesting photo-responsive behavior are particularly attractive in reversible gas uptake [20]. One of the well-known PMOFs was reported by Park et al., and they prepared a $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF with a photo-responsive ligand 2- (phenyldiazenyl)terephthalate. Upon irradiation, steric hindrance of azobenzene molecules will change the pore width freely and regulate the adsorption of carbon dioxide [26]. Similarly, Prasetya et al. utilized 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane as the ligand to synthesize a new Zn-azo-dabco MOF. The hindered isomerization of azobenzene molecules occupied in framework could also realize tailorable carbon dioxide adsorption [27]. However, the regulation of adsorption capacity of most reported PMOFs is based on steric hindrance or polarity transformation triggered by photoactive moieties owing to the weak interactions between adsorbent and adsorbate. This can be demonstrated by the linear adsorption isotherms presented in these studies. For practical applications, selective adsorption of guest molecules with high capacity is expected, for example, selective capture of aromatic sulfur compounds in ADS. Therefore, it is desirable to introduce specific active sites (such as $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ sites） into PMOFs to strengthen adsorbent-adsorbate interaction and exploit the regulation of active sites for ADS by configurational transformation of azobenzenes. Nevertheless, the regulation of strong interaction rather than weak physical interaction through isomerization of azobenzene is challenging.
Herein,for the first time,we report a PMOF for tailorable benzothiophene capture originating from the interplay between specific active sites $\mathrm{(Cu_{2}O)}$ and photo-responsive substrates (azobenzene). The photo-switchable adsorbents (azobenzenefunctionalized HKUST-1 (HK-Azo)) decorated with $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}O}$ were fabricated by adding azobenzene-containing ligands during HKUST-1 synthesis (Scheme 1). Thanks to the $\pi\cdot$ -complexation interaction of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ with aromatic sulfur compounds [28, 29],HKAzo exhibits  obviously larger  adsorption capacity for benzothiophene than parent HKUST-1. Meanwhile, the present HK-Azo is quite different from conventional PMOFs with adjustable adsorption capacity triggered by structural variation. Through irradiation with ultraviolet (UV） or visible (Vis） light, cis/trans isomerization of azobenzene molecules occur, resulting in shelter or exposure of active $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{+}}}$ sites, which successfully regulates benzothiophene adsorption. The calculation based on density functional theory (DFT） illustrates that once azobenzene moieties transfer from trans to cis configuration, surface electrostatic potential of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ decreases, leading to the shield of active sites. The HK-Azo exhibits adsorption capacity of benzothiophene up to $0.711\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ which is more than twice as much that of HKUST-1 $(0.344\ \mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ . Furthermore, the change of benzothiophene uptake is up to $29.7\%$ in HK-Azo upon cis/trans isomerization of azobenzene, while the change in HKUST-1 is negligible under the same conditions. This indicates that strong active sites for ADS responsive to light are successfully fabricated in MOFs.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Chemicals
Dichloromethane $\mathrm{(DCM,\>\99.5\%}$ ， anhydrous ethanol (EtOH, $>99.5\%$ ， anhydrous methanol $(\mathrm{MeOH},~>~99.5\%)$ ，N,Ndimethylformamide $(\mathrm{DMF},>99.5\%)$ ,and tetrahydrofuran (THF, $>99.5\%$ ）were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Cooperation. 1,3,5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC},\:>\:99\%)$ ， dimethyl 5-aminoisophthalate $(>99\%)$ ，p-toluidine $(>~99\%)$ benzothiophene $(>99\%)$ ， $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(>99\%)$ ,and isooctane $(>99\%)$ were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Co. Ltd. Oxone $(>99\%)$ ，anhydrous $\mathrm{MgSO_{4}}$ $(>99\%)$ ，chloroform $(>99\%)$ ，ethyl acetate $(>99\%)$ , hexane $(>99\%)$ ),KOH $(>99.5\%)$ ,and HCl $(36\%)$ were obtained from Adamas Reagent Co. Ltd. All chemicals were used as received without further purification.
## 2.2 Preparations of HKUST-1
HKUST-l was prepared under solvothermal conditions following the reported method [30]. $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ 1 $(105\mathrm{~mg},\ 0.5\mathrm{~mmol})$ was dissolved in a mixed solution containing DMF $\mathrm{~\textit~{~S~}~m L}$ ）and anhydrous ethanol ( $\mathrm{\Delta}5\mathrm{\mL},$ ）by ultrasonication for about $5~\mathrm{min}$ $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(217~\mathrm{mg},0.9~\mathrm{mmol})$ was dissolved in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ under ultrasonication for another $5~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the copper solution was slowly added to $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ solution with stirring at ambient temperature and heated at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~7~h~}$ under static conditions. After cooling to room temperature, the obtained blue crystal was washed with DMF and ethanol during a week and activated at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for $24\mathrm{h}$ to get pure sample.
## 2.3 Preparations of HK-Azo(x)
$\mathrm{HK}{\cdot}\mathrm{Azo}(x)$ was synthesized and treated following the same procedure as that for HKUST-l except for the mixed ligands used with different molar ratios of Azo- $_{\mathrm{BDC:H_{3}B T C}}$ $_{\mathrm{{AZO-BDC}}=5}$ -(ptolyldiazenyl） isophthalic acid). The obtained materials were denoted as $\mathrm{HK-Azo}(x)$ ，where $x$ is the proportion of Azo-BDC in dual ligands, with values of 10, 20, and 30.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Fabrication and characterization of the PMOFs
X-ray diffraction (XRD) was employed to confirm the phase purity of as-synthesized materials, and the result is given in Fig. 1(a). The diffraction peaks of HK-Azo are consistent with the simulated HKUST-1, which indicates the high crystallinity and typical topological structure same as HKUST-1. Meanwhile, compared with initial HKUST-l, two obvious characteristic diffraction peaks have emerged. The new peaks at $36.4^{\circ}$ and $42.3^{\circ}$ are associated with crystalline $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ (PDF#71-3645）[33]. Moreover, with the addition of Azo-BDC, the intensity of two peaks is increasing regularly, suggesting the more ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ sites existed in the skeleton. What's more, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) provides powerful evidence for the existence of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ According to XPS full spectra, element of $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}$ could be clearly detected (Fig.S8 in the ESM). Then analysis of $\mathrm{Cu}\ 2\mathrm{p}$ energy spectrum was proceeded to explore detailed valence state of copper in materials. For pure HKUST-l, the peak at binding energy of $934.7~\mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ along with a tiny shoulder peak at $932.4~\mathrm{eV}$ which is attributed to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{+}}}$ [34], suggesting the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites are dominant in the HKUST-1 (Fig. 1(b)). Nevertheless, the more addition of Azo-BDC, the stronger intensity of $932.4\mathrm{eV}$ is observed, revealing the portion of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ is enhanced. As a result, the content of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{+}}}$ is reaching up to $17.2\%$ 。 $27.1\%$ and $38.5\%$ based on peak fitting, corresponding to HK-Azo(l0), HKAzo(20), and HK-Azo(30), respectively. In high resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM） image, some nanoparticles with uniform size are dispersed in the channel and confirm that the clear lattice fringes with a lattice spacing of $0.305~\mathrm{nm}$ (PDF #7l-3645) corresponding to a stack of the [110] planes of the $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ lattice [35] (Fig.2(b)). Therefore, doping defective ligands during HKUST-1 synthesis procedure will successfully lead to mixed valence of $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/C u^{+}}$ .The $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ unit coordinates with four carboxylate acid groups of BTC in HKUST1, resulting in the formation of coordinaton saturated $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{4}$ paddlewheel coordination units. However, some of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ unit will not be coordinated with carboxylate group when Azo-BDC ligand with only two carboxylate groups was used to partially replace BTC ligand possessing three carboxylate acid groups, resulting in the formation of coordinatively unsaturated metal sites [36]. On the coordinatively unsaturated metal sites, copper ions with the assistance of redox active species (ethanol) in growth solution are selectively deposited to turn into copper atoms [37]. When deposited copper atoms reach a supersaturated state, the atoms begin to aggregate into small atomic clusters through selfnucleation or homogeneous nucleation, and the crystal nuclei in the solution begin to increase. With the increase of atomic supply, the nuclei begin to grow into nanoparticles through aggregation, which tends to form small $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}O}}$ nanoparticles.
The overall morphology of the materials was studied by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). All synthesized HK-Azo system exhibits stable structure and same octahedron particles like HKUST-1 (Fig.S9 in the ESM). To obtain an insight into the elemental distribution, the transmission_ electron microscopy (TEM)-mapping analysis was also conducted. The mapping results illustrate the elements of C, N, O, and Cu are finely distributed in HK-Azo(20) (Fig. 2(c)). To study the porosity of the materials, standard $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ were presented. HKUST-l shows a typical isotherm of type I and the rapid $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake at low pressure implies existence of rich micropores (Fig. 1(c)）[38,39]. Different to HKUST-1,HK-Azo all exhibits the pattern same as type IV and hysteresis loop at relative high pressures according to isotherms. The results mean mesopores were formed through mixed-ligand strategy [40, 41] and abundant mesopores are beneficial for mass transfer. With the increased introduction of defective linkers (Azo-BDC） in HK-Azo, the hysteresis loop in the adsorption-desorption pattern was converted from H2 type (bottle neck mesopore) to Hl type (cylindrical channels). The transformation of hysteresis loop illustrates a clear proof for increasing the mesopore window [42]. In HRTEM image of HK-Azo, the illustrated rough interface is also evidence for the formation of mesopores (Fig.2(a)). The incorporation of Azo-BDC creates defects, while the other parts of the framework remain intact.With the increase in Azo-BDC, the coordination between $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ unit and Azo-BDC was more prone to result in the formation of more defects. The adjacent pores could form mesopores caused by partially collapse of the framework. However, it is difficult to give an accurate value of the defects owing to the formation of large mesopores. For these samples, the amounts of defects could be reflected by mesopore volume, which is helpful to get more specific information (Table S1 in the ESM). Based on the calculation from Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) analysis which is commonly used to evaluate the pore size distribution of mesoporous materials [43], the mesopore volume of HK-Azo(10) is $0.28~\mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ . For HK-Azo(20) and HK-Azo(30), the mesopore volume is 0.39 and $0.32~\mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ ，respectively (Table S1 in the ESM). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) indicates the thermal stability of HK-Azo is slightly weaken compared with initial HKUST-l and all new synthesized materials could still maintain rigidity of structure (Fig. Si0 in the ESM).
