## 3.3 ADS performance of the PMOFs
The obtained PMOFs decorating $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{+}}}$ sites were employed for ADS, and a typical aromatic sulfur compound, benzothiophene, with the molecular size of $6.5\mathrm{~\AA~}\times\mathrm{~8~\overset{\circ}{\cdot}~\overset{\circ}{A}~}$ was chosen as the adsorbate [44]. Adsorption curves of benzothiophene performed at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Fig.Sl2 in the ESM. When adsorption process reaches equilibrium, all HK-Azo MOFs exhibit superior ADS performance compared with parent HKUST-1. Even it possesses high BET surface area $1{,}544\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ，the adsorption capacity of benzothiophene over HKUST-1 is only $0.344\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ Because HKUST-1 is a three-dimensional (3D) system containing a generic pore $(8\mathrm{~\AA~}\times8\mathrm{~\AA~})$ [40,45], such microporous structure may limit the adsorption of large sized aromatic  sulfur compounds. In the case of HK-Azo(l0), the adsorption capacity on benzothiophene is $0.537\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ .When the amounts of AzoBDC ligands reach $20\%$ ，HK-Azo(20) shows the highest sulfur adsorption capacity $(0.711\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}^{\cdot}$ ), which is higher than the HKAzo(l0) (Fig.4(a)). HK-Azo(20) possesses more mesopores (the pore volume of mesopore is about $0.39\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) and the amount of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ is about $27.1\%$ (Table S1 in the ESM), which is higher than HK-Azo(l0) even though the specific surface area is less than HKAzo(10). While for HK-Azo(30), capture of benzothiophene drops to $0.625\mathrm{mmol{g}^{-1}}$ owing to the less mesopores even if it possesses the highest content of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ . The result illustrates that the amounts of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ active sites and mesopores have synergic effect on adsorption capacity. We also compare the adsorption capacity of theoptimum material HK-Azo(20) with that reported in literature.The adsorption capacity of HK-Azo(20) $(0.711\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ is obviously superior to typical reported adsorbents including activated carbons $(0.28\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}^{\cdot}$ )，zeolites $(0.40\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ ，and MOFs $(0.25{-}0.57\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}^{})$ ）(Table S2 in the ESM).
The controllable adsorptive desulfurization performance was realized by light irradiation. It is well known that some materials could aggregate heat by adsorbing light energy, trigger a sharp rise in temperature, and act as photothermal adsorbents to release adsorbed molecules [46]. In our research, it is crucial to exclude the interference of photothermal effect, and make sure that photo isomerism of azobenzene group plays a leading role. Therefore, we monitor the temperature change of these materials under UV and Vis light irradiation for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ It demonstrates that for HKUST-1, HK-Azo(l0), HK-Azo(20), and HK-Azo(30), the temperatures of all materials are almost constant after light irradiation (Fig.Sl3 in the ESM). The above results reveal that when MOFs are exposed to light, they just undergo reversible photoisomerization rather than photothermic. At the beginning, even though HKUST-1 was exposed to UV light for a long time, benzothiophene uptake would barely change compared with Vis irradiation (Fig.S14 in the ESM). Nevertheless, all HK-Azo MOFs appeared a certain decrease in adsorption quantity during UV irradiation process. For HK-Azo(l0), the adsorption capacity of benzothiophene fell from 0.537 to $0.481\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ by UV light irradiation and the change amount reached $10.6\%$ (Fig. S15 in the ESM). Similarly, when the introduction of Azo-BDC ligands was further increased, HK-Azo(20) could only capture $0.501\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ benzothiophene after UV light irradiation, which is obviously lower than that after Vis light irradiation $(0.711\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdotg}^{-1}}^{\cdot}$ ）(Fig. S16 in the ESM). This corresponds to the change amount of adsorption capacity up to $29.7\%$ The change amount is close to the data ( $33.7\%$ and $29.2\%$ difference in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity) reported in published literatures about gas adsorption regulation [47,48]. In the case of HK-Azo(30), the change in adsorption capacity after UV and Vis light irradiation is $22.2\%$ (Fig.S17 in the ESM),which is lower than that of HK-Azo(20). An in situ light irradiation experiment was designed to study the change of uptake in the adsorption process. After adsorption for $2~\mathrm{\min}.$ 、the HK-Azo(20） was immediately irradiated with UV light. As displayed in Fig.4(b), the adsorption capacity drops clearly. When it is switched to Vis light, the adsorption kinetic trend is consistent with the initial again. Such dynamic light source switching strongly proves that interaction between azobenzene and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ active sites could intelligentlycontroldesulfurizationperformance.Cyclic benzothiophene adsorption performance of HK-Azo(20) triggered by different light irradiation was also considered as depicted in Fig. 4(d). The adsorption processes were performed for four trans/cis isomerization cycles. It demonstrates that adsorption capacity can be fully reappeared, revealing the excellent reversibility of the present adsorbents. Besides, the XRD patterns show that the used HK-Azo(20) still remains similar diffraction peaks, indicating the stability of framework. Meanwhile, the peaks at $36.4^{\circ}$ and $\bar{4}2.3^{\circ}$ still appear, illustrating the stable existence of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{+}}}$ sites (Fig. S18 in the ESM). The $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ sites were stable without oxidation owing to the fact that the whole experiment was carried out in solvent phase, which can isolate HK-Azo(20) from oxygen.
The above results indicate that the interaction between actives sites $\mathrm{(Cu_{2}O)}$ and photoactive molecules (azobenzene） can be altered reversibly through light irradiation. As we all know,π- complexation adsorbents with specific sites $\left(\mathrm{Cu^{+}}\right)$ are capable to remove aromatic sulfur compounds efficiently. Therefore, arising the decrease of adsorption capacity by UV light irradiation is that cis-azobenzene molecules in framework have shelter effect on $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ active sites. Compared with initial trans configuration of azobenzene, cis-azobenzene groups  induced by  photo isomerization presented an angular geometry and a dipole moment of $3.0\mathrm{~D~}$ . The larger steric hindrance would hinder the interaction between $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ and benzothiophene. In consequence, the uptake of benzothiophene declined obviously. Nevertheless, once cis-azobenzene was restored to initial trans situation by Vis light irradiation, the trans-azobenzene in planarized structure would let more $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ nanoparticles be re-exposed in channels. As a result, benzothiophene molecules were easier to interact with $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ sites in trans situation than cis situation (Scheme 1). DFT calculations were applied to demonstrate the possible mechanism for such interaction when azobenzene groups are in different photo isomerization. As shown in Fig.Sl9 in the ESM, it is revealed the optimum adsorption configuration and surface electrostatic potential of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ unit cell and trans/cis azobenzene groups. The electronic potential of single Cu atom is $0.156~\mathrm{eV};$ which is the main adsorption sites for benzothiophene. Upon contacting azobenzene groups in trans isomerization, the potential decreases to 0.l49 eV. Once the azobenzene groups are transformed to cis isomerization, the value is further down to $0.142\mathrm{eV}.$ This calculation illustrates that cis-azobenzene prefers to interact with active Cu atom compared with trans-azobenzene and then limits the accessible space between $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ active sites and benzothiophene. Hence, $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ would be more difficult for adsorbing aromatic sulfur compounds. The adsorption results and DFT calculations show that the rational utilization of the interactions between the $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ and the azobenzene groups can lead to a controllable adsorption for benzothiophene triggered by light stimulation.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, a HK-Azo MOF system possessing both photoresponsive properties  and $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ active sites in the framework was successfully fabricated. Characterization results illustrate that the content of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ and channel environment can be adjusted by changing the molar ratio of two ligands. HK-Azo(20) exhibits the optimal adsorption performance for benzothiophene, which is more than two times higher than the parent HKUST-1. Our results illustrate that photo isomerization of azobenzene groups affects the surface electronic potential of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ units, leading to the change in affnity for benzothiophene. Upon UV and Vis irradiation, controllable capture of benzothiophene can be realized due to the shelter and exposure of azobenzene moieties on ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ sites caused by photo isomerization, leading to the change amount up to $29.7\%$ . Nevertheless, HKUST-1 still maintains initial adsorption capacity of benzothiophene under the same UV and Vis light irradiation conditions. After multiple cycles, HK-Azo(20) still shows excellent adsorption performance and reversibility. We demonstrate the successful regulation of strong adsorption active sites by light irradiation,which is hard to realize by traditional PMOF adsorbents based on the regulation of weak active sites. The present study might inspire the development of new energyefficient adsorption process regulated by light instead of conventional pressure and temperature swing adsorption technologies.
